answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8978fe72-cace-5644-a25c-ae941745e1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514774} :answer-field/id #uuid "b596270b-2f6f-5b28-a9f8-1ea62f75429c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514780}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68bf99a3-7fc9-5547-9120-f2c14067205b" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c577ee7f4171d488642f9c8f9c8e4204269b05620ed22eae91db72b1f931837.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/01/0148dd6296370b01a9decbee10a96765be9af88c2f1eb8b9137d59131082c36f.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=p+q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =1$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f1211d-1dc5-5d77-b204-566c3ce70b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f1211d-1dc5-5d77-b204-566c3ce70b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514776} :answer-field/id #uuid "0822b810-bc53-57e5-854c-5c75f04f62a9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 1759218