er-field/id #uuid "2f2b8574-b828-57ad-9236-3ab32e3e7989" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241831da-eaa7-564a-a150-2d9953025324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241831da-eaa7-564a-a150-2d9953025324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514755} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b2f95f-1ac6-5ddd-8eb5-57a7fdc6944b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514775}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b38e8849-a51d-558f-9b16-3471fb9b447e" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab91b3b28b656d65dedc23b8ca71d386ae9f46b0ebbb2ab6b815fd7b81cfc2ba.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/a4/a4cb7a15f5cb1b818899d388b393da81d8fc873d2da5a1888286dbe8a9ffc4fd.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{3}{2}|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\frac{3}{2}q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{11}{3}|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=-\\frac{11}{3}p.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}q-\\frac{11}{3}p \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{3}p+\\frac{3}{2}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-\\frac{11}{3}p+\\frac{3}{2}q.\n$$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{11}{3}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{3}{2}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "748f0d67-e453-56c2-87c0-cb5fdec04227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "748f0d67-e453-56c2-87c0-cb5fdec04227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514768} :answer-field/id #uuid "8333649f-2868-5786-9fff-a516d4b46c68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859cd3c8-1f31-5a52-b503-a5757580dbdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859cd3c8-1f31-5a52-b503-a5757580dbdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514772} :answer-field/id #uuid "8626d948-d0ea-5b0c-9a0e-6082d083ee80" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514773}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bbd1b0a1-ee60-5a86-a8ad-b5834136a338" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c34e3e967bbad961e3bd82514479ebfcc391a6e66801aeb40d44c56b49c6968.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/cc/ccbb0fd7c3314645d1563a0b70a916b2008e3e6125242bbb9094d5de8da26950.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{4}{3}|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\frac{4}{3}q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{15}{4}|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=-\\frac{15}{4}p.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}q-\\frac{15}{4}p \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}p+\\frac{4}{3}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-\\frac{15}{4}p+\\frac{4}{3}q.\n$$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{15}{4}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{4}{3}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e17a768d-2790-5422-bb4b-bdf82e397acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e17a768d-2790-5422-bb4b-bdf82e397acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514786} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0f7042-5563-51f7-9cab-942bf39cc5ad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f841a8-0bb6-58e4-b938-cdeb3f547958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49f841a8-0bb6-58e4-b938-cdeb3f547958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514761} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4591dc0-49da-57f1-b9b6-9e5cf94e5e9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea6bc20e-dd45-51a3-a105-158a9f1b063a" :question/problem "![](images/11/1129eb74332cdea7d149aedf6b03abad182ed75d8d5d802ba15ff2503a816c55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/2e/2e22361f45d4f9c98c2558c298fbcddfda78e205ab2ab39f9ee9dd7a534670a2.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{4}{3}|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-\\frac{4}{3}q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{11}{3}|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=\\frac{11}{3}p.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}q+\\frac{11}{3}p \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}p-\\frac{4}{3}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=\\frac{11}{3}p-\\frac{4}{3}q.\n$$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =\\frac{11}{3}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{4}{3}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35315760-1bb0-518b-b5fb-e20fed2adc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35315760-1bb0-518b-b5fb-e20fed2adc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514757} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a49d3fe-0fc8-5769-abe9-5af9449db940" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4df125ee-ac0c-579f-a1cf-a41d2e271165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4df125ee-ac0c-579f-a1cf-a41d2e271165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514762} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba69f6e-d62b-5869-9322-3eab8daf66d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb7bb682-766d-55d0-a9e3-945d7f1dcea9" :question/problem "![](images/06/060c4e83c464cfcd60303e524b142bdcc60e9a05125361d84babcdc4bbc79b41.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/d7/d7d81b7870205f722a1cc3d23131e573ead2cdfbc66ffcef81d75f31eac6c458.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=p+2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0830e01b-93be-55a3-ac87-38d68196c9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572323} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd72a4f-8a2a-5d84-a5df-17bc127c8a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572348} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed3d896-e6f3-5e0d-9156-7213b2534219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572467} {:answer/id #uuid "a2644a20-0b59-59e2-bf9a-fb12db4f0be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572490} {:answer/id #uuid "a306cce2-e35e-56e8-a262-e2dc8808954f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0830e01b-93be-55a3-ac87-38d68196c9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572323} :answer-field/id #uuid "3810e182-6568-500c-b95b-e07cc9fea34d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572610}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ada3d5e7-07f2-5981-9fcd-59fe51195895" :question/math-academy-id "q-120273" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dea155246de4f35697780db7ebf1802cdf5c2899fe42fa494993be2f65dd7f3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/99/9905448ad59cb2f68f5def832811eb34056305d1a535f23387f4403a340cb8f8.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=\\frac{1}{2}|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=-\\frac{1}{2}f.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $g$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-2g.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8cd8a3-5848-5337-929a-567d0fe4c28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572355} {:answer/id #uuid "58536380-845e-5010-b39b-f5e39e4c551c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572422} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a75f3a-4920-5e33-be0c-78e376d995c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572497} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1a8c24-4115-5d89-a601-bd095e5bfa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572523} {:answer/id #uuid "e054b3a2-0eea-5210-8737-47555b3de52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a75f3a-4920-5e33-be0c-78e376d995c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572497} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e94447c-6428-5f0d-9958-0d3458e2b5ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572623}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeb1828d-7e99-5d6a-aa95-0e286af84c7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120265" :question/problem "![](images/67/6715bda9432c2ba3e61c31f436cd6b7608312da499bb4372944d25db6cf76090.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/89/8997fda665e4bba248dd19f5b8fe0d2a2524df341042271a0f8426e0ce152ecd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05da8884-0b84-53b4-be8d-0c4ee296a4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581860} {:answer/id #uuid "5518a4f0-c680-5b9b-baf1-dcd20aab042e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581971} {:answer/id #uuid "84331dd6-6bc5-518f-b9ff-e7302d0e4b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582042} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd54c5f-bc9e-5214-9bad-257eb4fdb6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582086} {:answer/id #uuid "a8db350d-738f-580d-959b-caceb8c956c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fd54c5f-bc9e-5214-9bad-257eb4fdb6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582086} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33f9199-1c48-55ab-bf55-87ed20786849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582303}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a51e863-8cb5-5ea3-be4b-fcc4293d84e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120275" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbd0ffe0fb9e879f2a93557da1aef08622c9e048e14d83c4f4619cf5a1cd47bb.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fba4b74fde44c862267cd2c58852f4f829f5b07ead948b0c18d79e94f7d1c0fc.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ and $u$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=2u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=3|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-3v.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =2u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v=2u-3v.$\n\nSince $u∦v,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f01f5e69-1133-5d92-a2b1-b7709d266f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f01f5e69-1133-5d92-a2b1-b7709d266f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735011} :answer-field/id #uuid "d840e498-dfe3-51b4-9ccd-ed6271151a02" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4be812c-9f7d-59cd-90cd-daaecd99c55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4be812c-9f7d-59cd-90cd-daaecd99c55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735009} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbbec0e8-517e-5143-9c7c-3445f3efbc99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c62a9e0e-7283-5db6-8603-4fc28da1e7a9" :question/problem "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram on a square grid](images/da/da826185105dfc1ec4e45b6b32c7a7da6da33bb1a27aed665f9b67fadae4128c.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "![Completed vector path](images/c2/c2bcb1d71d99d24ae325386c8b99146b5854c6186b0f25ce64bfe24cf8379ef8.png)\n\nConstruct a route $O\\to X\\to C$ whose first segment is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and whose second segment is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$. The added dashed lines show this route.\n\nA copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves one square right and two squares up. The segment from $O$ to $X$ moves three squares right and six squares up, so $OX=3|\\mathbf{b}|$ with the same direction. Thus $\\overrightarrow{OX}=3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nA copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves two squares right and one square up. From $X$ to $C$, move four squares left and two squares down. The length is $2|\\mathbf{a}|$, but the direction is opposite, so $\\overrightarrow{XC}=-2\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, this expression is unique. Match each coefficient to its vector, rather than to the order of travel: $\\lambda=-2$ and $\\mu=3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122433f9-4f97-5f10-b51e-cae32384b524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "122433f9-4f97-5f10-b51e-cae32384b524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735000} :answer-field/id #uuid "20403580-572a-5e11-89dc-40453809e0ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a5c521-ee60-5fca-9da6-55ee3551aee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5a5c521-ee60-5fca-9da6-55ee3551aee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735006} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9ce837f-c214-5fa2-af1b-b6c1b4ed8c37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d46199db-7d10-55ea-8f4c-bb6ccaaae660" :question/problem "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram on a square grid](images/c6/c6bc3baa512da1ef4f9fffc94d0f1069402f58828a9b8ff732a6b97b1543597e.png)\n\nThe dashed route goes from $O$ to $A$ to $C$. Use the grid to compare each part with $\\mathbf{a}$ or $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "![Completed vector path](images/5d/5d7bbadbc141798871c16c1175696cf27628e2966c84d2bb2f744ff65d527c71.png)\n\nFollow the supplied route $O\\to A\\to C$. On the grid, $OA$ is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}$ and points in the same direction, so $\\overrightarrow{OA}=2\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nThe segment from $A$ to $C$ moves six squares right and three squares down. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves two squares right and one square down. Thus $AC=3|\\mathbf{b}|$, with the same direction, and $\\overrightarrow{AC}=3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83aa297f-c7f9-5297-b7ee-0b257f47927b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83aa297f-c7f9-5297-b7ee-0b257f47927b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735004} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a075dfb-65b3-55a4-a702-e92f22aba919" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186734999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30a82fd8-72e7-5e30-8121-4b7dc2972576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30a82fd8-72e7-5e30-8121-4b7dc2972576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735002} :answer-field/id #uuid "6280df77-f517-5e12-9966-be78f6d11a31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735003}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdf23a75-ec61-5ac9-a21d-3757ff6f8cb1" :question/problem "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram on a square grid](images/a4/a4e769a145f41df882458202f1e9540ea17580e3bd661236cd334c8c68478133.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "![Completed vector path](images/be/be23bdf90ef85ad1d8b373ca25d9112b6d574c316f729c90db4269bdc3984147.png)\n\nDraw a path $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$, as shown.\n\nFrom $O$ to $X$, move three squares down. The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ moves two squares up, so $OX=\\frac{3}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the directions are opposite. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nFrom $X$ to $C$, move four squares right and two squares up. This is twice the displacement of $\\mathbf{b}$, in the same direction, so $\\overrightarrow{XC}=2\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two reference vectors are not parallel, so matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\mu=2$. The negative sign records the downward part of the route; a length ratio alone does not give its sign."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bafc4198-e4dd-5be3-8eef-1544cf656974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bafc4198-e4dd-5be3-8eef-1544cf656974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735257} :answer-field/id #uuid "67af80ae-b5e2-5643-b036-bd4bfa9471d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735250} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5551e412-bb88-508f-b002-4209bc9a6198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5551e412-bb88-508f-b002-4209bc9a6198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735246} :answer-field/id #uuid "df797a40-f4c2-5376-85ec-b5e6ffb94002" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735259}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03cb3efc-e3ab-52b7-b9e6-28cbeee3b50c" :question/problem "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/b8/b87f77266a2d44642d01744a5bdcecf42345dd5ffd26531eae5af7c77bea2722.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "![Completed vector route](images/90/9013b552fbf1df93e6872f3fd213f1c80e3d4c1e935dd2317f04d52b69d3cdd2.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $12$ squares down. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $4$ squares up. Thus $OX=3|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $3$ squares left and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares up. Thus $XC=\\frac{3}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=-3\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\mu=-3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6554a1-f893-59fa-b799-8615c07ba1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6554a1-f893-59fa-b799-8615c07ba1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735248} :answer-field/id #uuid "15939a3f-578b-5711-ac13-9f83da140649" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ece49d-d9d1-5f19-8426-22db32ca5570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11ece49d-d9d1-5f19-8426-22db32ca5570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735242} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5243efd-720b-5e72-b636-4cf0286f5dbf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735260}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "628e7c86-511a-5a96-a42d-cd88be4d4559" :question/problem "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/6f/6f39fa0f0006ca782883fcd6ce5f5f687e8ec90cceb15d1cc5eda21cd0ba4c14.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "![Completed vector route](images/62/62405de33e197db1e8ef48b2d39c5909179e00c9b933cb8b2820101f0323cbb9.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $6$ squares left and $6$ squares down. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares right and $2$ squares up. Thus $OX=3|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $1$ square up. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square up. Thus $XC=1|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=-3\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}\\\\&=\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=1$ and $\\mu=-3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a080f58e-4c64-5ebd-8425-6724a800df69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a080f58e-4c64-5ebd-8425-6724a800df69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735256} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e3a506c-701e-5999-b79a-a6e1b6d3da3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735251} {:answer-field/choices [{